            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A typological representation of death, the grave (Sheol), and the tomb of Christ, serving as an instrument of both divine judgment and miraculous preservation leading to resurrection.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17\",\"Jonah 2:1-2\",\"Matthew 12:40\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A representation of primordial chaos, evil, or demonic forces (such as Leviathan or Rahab) that God conquers and subdues.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 27:1\",\"Psalm 74:13-14\",\"Job 41:1-34\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A symbol of hostile Gentile empires (like Babylon or Assyria) that swallow Israel in exile and are forced by God to release a remnant.\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 51:34-44\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"An emblem of God's absolute sovereignty and providence over the natural world, acting as an obedient agent of His will.\",\"basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17\",\"Jonah 2:10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In Christian theology, the Great Fish is most famously linked to the Sign of Jonah mentioned by Jesus, making it a prominent type for Christ's burial and resurrection. When broadly translated as a sea-monster (tannin), it frequently overlaps with ancient Near Eastern motifs of chaos and evil empires subdued by Yahweh.\"}",
